    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/420766250/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """good. Still, perhaps it is a little advance to force one’s self to go and say the words, even when one can’t help feelings of despair and hopelessness lurking behind. Another time perhaps I shall get further, and be able to put myself into Bernhard’s place. 
Wednesday Nov. 17. 1897. Villa Rosa. Fiesole
Bernhard had his massage, and then called on Miss Blood, who told him he had made an unfavorable impression on Mme. de Montebello and the Princess Ghika the night he dined there because he spoke slightingly of Amiel and Marie Bashkirtseff,  whom they were praising. I had the day alone, practising and being unhappy. I read Dr. Bucke’s  Man’s Moral Nature . Bernhard is reading  Lucretius  and  Pindar . """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 164 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1896-98> .

